        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(ai): add handshake timeout to ChatGPT Responses WebSocket connect",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where `connectWebSocket()` in the ChatGPT Responses provider\ncould hang indefinitely when the remote server accepts the TCP connection but\nnever completes the HTTP WebSocket upgrade.\n\n`connectWebSocket()` uses `globalThis.WebSocket` (standard WebSocket API),\nwhich does **not** support the `ws`-library `handshakeTimeout` option, and the\nfunction had no `setTimeout`-based fallback. When the caller does not set\n`options.timeoutMs`, the entire signal chain (buildRequestSignal →\ncreateFirstStreamEventAbortController) has no timeout, and the SSE fallback at\nline 374 of `streamOpenAICodexResponses` never triggers because it requires the\nWebSocket promise to reject first.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdded a `setTimeout`-based handshake deadline inside `connectWebSocket()`.\nThe deadline applies unconditionally as a connection-phase timeout:\n\n- **No `options.timeoutMs`**: 30s default (matches Discord/Signal/QQBot)\n- **`options.timeoutMs` set**: forwarded as `handshakeTimeoutMs` — the caller's\n  explicit budget governs the handshake phase\n- **Cancellation-only signal + no `timeoutMs`**: 30s fallback still applies\n\nThe timer and the signal's `onAbort` handler race cleanly via the `settled`\nflag: whichever fires first settles the promise and `cleanup()` prevents the\nother from leaking or double-rejecting.\n\nThe effective timeout value is now reflected in the error message instead of\nthe hardcoded constant.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers will no longer experience indefinite hangs when the OpenAI ChatGPT\nResponses WebSocket server accepts TCP but delays or never completes the HTTP\nupgrade. Users who configured a custom `timeoutMs` value will have that budget\nrespected as the handshake deadline.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Unit tests** (new file, 6 tests, all passing):\n  - Default 30s timer when no handshakeTimeoutMs\n  - Explicit handshakeTimeoutMs (5s) governs the deadline\n  - Timer still fires when a cancellation-only signal is present\n  - Normal open, error, and abort paths\n\n  ```\n  ✓ applies default 30s timer when no handshakeTimeoutMs is specified\n  ✓ uses caller-specified handshakeTimeoutMs when provided\n  ✓ timer still fires when a cancellation signal is present\n  ✓ resolves normally when WebSocket opens before timeout\n  ✓ rejects with error when WebSocket errors before timeout\n  ✓ rejects with abort error when signal aborts before timeout\n\n  Test Files  1 passed (1)\n       Tests  6 passed (6)\n  ```\n\n- **Real-connection proof**: loopback TCP server that accepts connections but\n  never sends the HTTP WebSocket upgrade, tested with `handshakeTimeoutMs=5000`\n  and **no** external AbortSignal — the built-in timer fires independently:\n\n  ```\n  === Handshake timeout proof ===\n  Server: TCP accept on :33459, no WebSocket upgrade\n  Server: TCP accepted, no WebSocket upgrade\n  Duration: 5.043s\n  Result: rejected — \"WebSocket connection to OpenAI Responses API timed out after 5000ms\"\n  ```\n\n  The duration (~5.04s) matches the configured 5000ms handshake timeout,\n  confirming the timer fires without any external signal or cancellation.\n\n- **Exit path coverage**: All 5 terminal paths (onOpen, onError, onClose,\n  onAbort, constructor catch) correctly clear the timer via `cleanup()`. The\n  `settled` flag prevents double-rejection if both the timer and an event fire.\n\n- **No regression**: Existing retry tests (5 tests) continue to pass.\n\n- **Test-only export**: `connectWebSocketForTest` exposes the internal function\n  for testing only, following the established `parseSSEForTest` pattern.\n\n- **Error message**: now shows the actual effective timeout value (e.g. 5000ms\n  when configured, not the hardcoded default constant).\n\n---\n\nAI-assisted.",
